Here's the proposed config. I haven't written it to disk.

```js
const authInternalImport = {
    selector: 'ImportDeclaration[source.value=/auth.internal/]',
    message: 'Import auth through src/auth/index.js, never its internals.',
};

const newDateNow = {
    selector: "NewExpression[callee.name='Date'][arguments.length=0]",
    message: 'Read the current time through the clock module, not new Date().',
};

export default [
    {
        files: ['**/*.{js,mjs,cjs}'],
        ignores: ['src/clock/**'],
        rules: {
            'no-restricted-syntax': ['error', newDateNow],
        },
    },
    {
        files: ['src/**/*.js'],
        ignores: ['src/auth/**'],
        rules: {
            'no-restricted-syntax': ['error', authInternalImport, newDateNow],
        },
    },
    {
        files: ['src/clock/**/*.js'],
        rules: {
            'no-restricted-syntax': ['error', authInternalImport],
        },
    },
];
```

**Why it has three blocks:** In ESLint's flat config, when two blocks set the same rule for a file, the later block's options replace the earlier ones. A single "ban `new Date()` everywhere" block would therefore either switch off the existing ban on importing auth internals for `src/**`, or be switched off by it. So the existing `src` block now has both selectors. The first block covers files outside `src` and inside `src/auth/**`. The third block lets the clock module call `new Date()` but keeps the auth import ban for it.

**Assumptions to confirm:**
- **Clock module location:** There's no clock module in the repo yet, so I assumed it will live at `src/clock/`. If it goes somewhere else, change that path in both places.
- **What gets banned:** It only blocks `new Date()` with no arguments, which is how code reads the current time. `new Date(ts)` and `new Date('2026-01-01')` just convert a value, so they still pass. To ban every `new Date(...)`, delete `[arguments.length=0]` from the selector.
- **Other ways to read the time:** `Date.now()` also reads the current time and isn't blocked. To close that gap, add `{ selector: "CallExpression[callee.object.name='Date'][callee.property.name='now']", message: ... }` to the same arrays.

**Current state of the repo:**
- Nothing calls `new Date(` right now, so the new rule produces no errors today.
- `npm run lint` already fails, but not because of this change: `src/billing/invoice.js:1` imports `../auth/internal/token.js`, which the existing auth rule forbids.

I haven't run ESLint against this config.
